    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "I think nested types needs to be managed. Following code:\n\n```cython\ncdef list[list[int]] a = [[1]]\nprint(cython.typeof(a.pop().pop()))\nprint(cython.typeof(a[0][0]))\n```\nIs printing, what is not optimal:\n```\nPython object\nint\n```\n\nUpdate:\n\nThe issue is not related to nesting types (the reproducer works even for `list[int]` type). The issue is that when `.pop()` is called, Cython handles return value via\n\nhttps://github.com/cython/cython/blob/87f82b7d7c84886396e15f0c09910011188047af/Cython/Compiler/ExprNodes.py#L6465\n\nThis function forces the return line to be pyobject:\n\nhttps://github.com/cython/cython/blob/87f82b7d7c84886396e15f0c09910011188047af/Cython/Compiler/ExprNodes.py#L6277-L6282\n\nI am inclined to leave this improvement to other PR...\n\n_Originally posted by @matusvalo in https://github.com/cython/cython/issues/7424#issuecomment-4364523936_\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This issue does not affects cases when assignment is used - hence:\n> ```python\n> b = a.pop().pop()\n>```\n> infers the type of `b` correctly \n            ",
    "closed_at": "2026-07-28T06:09:17Z",
    "closed_by": {
